**Simple solution.** One pass, O(n) time, O(1) space:
```java
static long count(String s, char c) {
    long n = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) if (s.charAt(i) == c) n++;
    return n;
}
```

**Parallel solution.** Counting is associative and independent per chunk, so split the string into T roughly equal ranges (T near the number of cores), have each worker count its range into a **local** variable, then sum the partial results.
```java
static long parallelCount(String s, char c, int t) throws Exception {
    ExecutorService ex = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(t);
    int len = s.length(), chunk = (len + t - 1) / t;
    List<Future<Long>> fs = new ArrayList<>();
    for (int i = 0; i < len; i += chunk) {
        final int lo = i, hi = Math.min(len, i + chunk);
        fs.add(ex.submit(() -> { long n = 0; for (int k = lo; k < hi; k++) if (s.charAt(k) == c) n++; return n; }));
    }
    long total = 0;
    for (Future<Long> f : fs) total += f.get();
    ex.shutdown();
    return total;
}
```
A parallel stream (`s.chars().parallel().filter(x -> x == c).count()`) does the same thing with less code. Do **not** use one shared counter with a lock for every match (heavy contention); local counts plus a final merge avoids it.

**Trade-offs.** Parallelism has overhead (threads, task splitting, merging), so it pays off only for very large inputs; for small ones the single thread is faster. A linear scan is often limited by memory bandwidth, not CPU, so speed-up flattens. If the string is too large for one machine's memory, process it in streamed chunks from disk or distribute chunks across machines (a map-reduce style job) and sum counts. Watch chunk boundaries only if you count patterns longer than one character.

**Other topics.** *Vertical scaling:* a bigger machine, simple, but has a ceiling and is a single point of failure. *Horizontal scaling:* more machines behind a load balancer, giving elasticity and fault tolerance but needing stateless services, data partitioning and coordination. *Relational vs NoSQL:* relational for transactions, joins and strong consistency; NoSQL for large-scale key-based access, flexible schema and horizontal scaling. *Redis vs Memcached:* Redis supports rich data types, persistence, replication, clustering, pub/sub and scripting; Memcached is a simple, multi-threaded in-memory key-value cache that is easy to run for plain caching.
